lesson.body:
Decomposition is the general strategy of breaking a large problem into smaller, more manageable parts. Functions are the basic unit Python gives for packaging a piece of code under its own name. A helper function is what happens when those two ideas meet: a small function pulled out specifically to do one job on behalf of a larger function, rather than cramming every step of a task into a single long block of code.

Consider a program that needs to clean up a list of names, keeping only the valid ones and formatting them consistently. Written with helper functions, the structure looks like this:

def is_valid_name(name):
    """Return True if name contains only letters and spaces."""
    return all(ch.isalpha() or ch.isspace() for ch in name)

def format_name(name):
    """Return name in Title Case with extra spaces stripped."""
    return name.strip().title()

def process_names(names):
    cleaned = []
    for name in names:
        if is_valid_name(name):
            cleaned.append(format_name(name))
    return cleaned

print(process_names(["  ana garcia ", "b0b", "chen wei"]))

Tracing process_names on this list: for "  ana garcia ", every character is either a letter or a space, so is_valid_name returns True, and format_name strips the surrounding spaces and title-cases it into "Ana Garcia". For "b0b", the character '0' is neither alphabetic nor a space, so is_valid_name returns False, and it gets skipped entirely. For "chen wei", the same check passes, and formatting produces "Chen Wei". The final printed result is ['Ana Garcia', 'Chen Wei'].

Notice how process_names itself reads almost like a description of the algorithm in plain language: for each name, if it is valid, format it and keep it. All the messy detail of what counts as valid, and what formatting actually means, is pushed down into two small, clearly named helpers, each with its own docstring stating exactly what it promises to do. Each helper can also be tested and fixed on its own; a bug in how validity is checked lives entirely inside is_valid_name and cannot be confused with a bug in formatting.

Helper functions also pay off through reuse. If another part of the program later needs to check whether a single name is valid, outside the context of processing a whole list, is_valid_name is already written, tested, and ready to call directly, rather than that logic being buried inside process_names where nothing else could reach it.

A good helper function generally does exactly one job, has a name that reads as a verb phrase describing that job, and is short enough to understand in a glance. If a function's purpose can only be described with "and", such as validate and format and save, that is usually a sign it should be split into separate helpers. Helper functions are also typically not meant to be called directly by whoever runs the finished program, only by other functions inside it, which sets up a natural question: what function does get called first, to start everything running. That is the role of the main function pattern, covered next.
